---
// Act Three — The World. Prose verbatim from /docs/01.
const VIDEO_SRC = "/mp4/SnapInsta.to_AQMLpm-xxb95KNPyM3VTISOp7s0_t20xwWF_w5j-7JWmUf4FueERGe9DHN61ONYA7L8d33JX68bxKxffTKAdbubLDufeyJPTmLo2SEw.mp4";
---

<section id="world" class="world">
  <div class="inner">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="meta" data-reveal>The World</p>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-reveal>
        There is a world most people <em>have never seen.</em>
      </h2>
    </header>

    <div class="body" data-reveal>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          It moves in a circle. Wellington in winter. Aachen in summer. The
          Hamptons in late August. Geneva by Christmas. The same eighty or so
          faces — the riders, the owners, the grooms, the families — show up at
          each stop, set up their barns, compete for millions, and leave for the
          next city. They live their entire lives inside this orbit. They call
          it <em>the circuit.</em>
        </p>

        <p>
          It is one of the wealthiest professional sports on earth, and one of
          the least televised. A single horse can sell for fifteen million
          dollars. A season campaigning at the top level costs more than a
          Premier League salary. The grooms who care for these animals know them
          better than any human in their lives. The riders are athletes, but
          they are also showmen, businesspeople, and in many cases, the children
          of the most powerful families in the world. Bruce Springsteen’s
          daughter is on the U.S. Olympic team. Bill Gates’ daughter married an
          Egyptian Olympian. Steve Jobs’ daughter is a working international
          rider. They compete against the working professionals who built their
          careers from nothing — McLain Ward, Kent Farrington, Laura Kraut —
          riders who have spent thirty years getting to where the heirs arrived
          as teenagers.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 2028, the Olympics come home. For the first time since 1984, the
          equestrian Games will be held on American soil, at Santa Anita Park in
          Arcadia, California. American equestrian sport has not produced an
          individual show jumping gold medalist since Joe Fargis won in Los Angeles
          forty-four years earlier. Boyd Martin is chasing the first US individual
          eventing gold in over two decades. Every rider on the circuit — American,
          European, Middle Eastern, South American — is now building toward one
          stadium and a single weekend in July 2028.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>The Circuit</em> is an unscripted cinematic series in the lineage of
          <em>The Last Dance</em>, <em>Free Solo</em>, and <em>Buck</em> — real
          people, real barns, real horses, real stakes. It moves at the speed of
          a season, a career, a horse’s lifetime.
        </p>

        <p>
          The circuit is three worlds at once. The show jumpers chase prize money
          and podiums across the LGCT and the World Cup circuit. The dressage
          riders move through a slower, more internal world of partnership and
          precision, where the most celebrated horse alive can have a state funeral
          when she retires. The eventers live closest to the sport’s oldest
          question: what does it cost a human to ask this of an animal?
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="video-aside">
        <video class="aside-video" muted loop playsinline preload="none" data-lazy-video>
          <source data-src={VIDEO_SRC} type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="pull" data-reveal>
      The horse is the only Olympic athlete that doesn’t know it’s competing.
      That, more than anything, is what makes this sport <em>cinema.</em>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .world {
    padding: var(--section-y) var(--gutter);
    background: var(--color-paper-warm);
  }
  .inner {
    max-width: 80rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }
  .body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 260px;
    gap: 4rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .prose p {
    margin-bottom: 1.5em;
    font-size: clamp(1.0625rem, 1rem + 0.3vw, 1.1875rem);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-ink-soft);
  }
  .prose p:first-child::first-line {
    font-variant: small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .prose em {
    font-style: italic;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "opsz" 14, "SOFT" 50, "WONK" 1;
    color: var(--color-bay);
  }
  .video-aside {
